{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"legend","type":"text","text":"**How to read**\n⬇ **Down = progress**: the steps in the order we did them.\n➡ **Right = depth**: what a step found, the decision on it, and where the route ends.\n\n**Legend**\n🟪 problem · 🟦 step\n🟩 decision / conclusion\n🟨 open\n\n**Where a route ends**\n🟥 ⛔ **Dead end**: tried, nothing left to explore\n🟧 ⏸ **Stopped**: could work, but not worth the cost (for now)\n\n✓ chosen · ✗ didn't work","x":-460,"y":0,"width":340,"height":470},
		{"id":"g0","type":"text","x":0,"y":0,"width":520,"height":578,"color":"6","text":"# Document growth: keeping search accurate as the corpus grows\nThe corpus (512k company documents today) keeps growing. Any per-document processing (summaries, facts, labels, buckets) must:\n- work for every new document, cheaply;\n- not lower the accuracy for documents already indexed.\n\n**Why it's its own problem:** the LLM-summary route for semantic search was stopped partly because accuracy on older documents can't be guaranteed as new ones arrive (see the semantic search failures map, step 5)."},
		{"id":"n17","type":"text","text":"## 1 · Bucket the corpus\n**Idea:** facts don't separate near-duplicates well enough. Instead, split the 512k documents into buckets (topic → project → feature), like an index note that links every note with a one-line description. The agent reads the index, picks buckets, and searches only inside them. Scales as long as the buckets are good.\n\n**Example (qst_0178):** the topic (quantization) covers 108k docs; the feature name (\"precision annealing\") narrows it to 7.","x":0,"y":718,"width":520,"height":400,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"n18","type":"text","text":"## 1a · Paper: TnT-LLM (Microsoft, 2024)\n[arXiv 2403.12173](https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.12173)\n\n**What it does:** an LLM summarizes every sampled item, builds a taxonomy from the summaries in many batches, then labels data; a small classifier trained on those labels sorts the rest.\n\n- ✓ Same pattern as our idea: a big model builds the taxonomy, a small model assigns at scale.\n- ✗ Needs an LLM summary per item and taxonomy passes over a large share of the data: far too many tokens for us.","x":600,"y":718,"width":520,"height":430,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"n19","type":"text","text":"### ✓ Decision (9 Nov)\nDon't use TnT-LLM as is. Keep its pattern, replace the expensive steps:\n- **Cluster** the gte vectors first (free); the LLM only **names ~2,000 clusters** from ~10 titles each (≈ 4M tokens, ≈ $3).\n- **Titles** instead of LLM summaries (summaries stopped, see step 5 of the semantic search failures map).\n- Taxonomy from a **small sample**; a small model or vector similarity assigns the rest (TopicGPT: open models assign well, but create taxonomies poorly).","x":1200,"y":718,"width":520,"height":400,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"n20","type":"text","text":"### ? Next\n**Free check:** cluster the corpus and see whether each of the 46 failures' right document sits in a small cluster with its look-alikes (e.g. qst_0178 in a \"precision annealing\" cluster, not among 108k quantization docs).\n\n**Watch:** when new documents arrive, the buckets must stay accurate for the old ones. A fixed taxonomy lowers this risk but doesn't remove it, so test adding documents too.","x":1800,"y":718,"width":520,"height":420,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"end6a","type":"text","text":"### ⏸ Stopped: TnT-LLM end to end\nPer-item LLM summaries plus repeated taxonomy passes cost too many tokens on 512k documents.\n\n**Reopen if** LLM tokens get much cheaper.","x":1200,"y":1198,"width":520,"height":250,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"g2","type":"text","x":0,"y":1588,"width":520,"height":424,"color":"5","text":"## 2 · Keyword routing (9 Nov)\n**Idea:** no clustering; a small model labels the data.\n1. The SLM writes keywords for each document (feature, project, concept; not names or ids).\n2. An LLM deduplicates them into one canonical list, with a mapping.\n3. Store each keyword, its embedding and its documents.\n4. Search the keywords by meaning; they **route** to a small group, and the reranker ranks inside it."},
		{"id":"g2a","type":"text","x":600,"y":1588,"width":520,"height":298,"color":"4","text":"### ✓ Why it fits growth\n- A new document only gets keywords from the SLM and is mapped onto the existing list. Old documents are never re-labelled.\n- Two keywords found to be the same later → one row in the mapping.\n- Small: tens of thousands of keyword vectors, not millions."},
		{"id":"g2b","type":"text","x":1200,"y":1588,"width":520,"height":328,"color":"3","text":"### ? Watch / next\n- Keywords must be **feature-level**: a topic like \"quantization\" (108k docs) routes nowhere.\n- Very common keywords and names are noise.\n- The question must be turned into keyword phrases first.\n- **Free test:** with the DeepSeek entities of the 46 failures' candidates, does the question reach a small group that holds the right document?"}
	],
